  "response": "## The most dangerous thing in the repo: `sendPayout` in `src/legacy/payouts.ts`\n\nThe scan lists it as `crime_00009` (\"Double Jeopardy\", unsafe retry), ninth of 29 and only **medium** severity. I think it's the worst finding anyway. I read the source to check, and the code is worse than the scan's evidence suggests.\n\n### Evidence\n\nFrom the scan:\n> `retry construct: attempt loop: while (attempt < 5) (line 25)` · `retried mutation — fetch at line 28 (HTTP POST)` · `missing safeguard (primary): no idempotency or deduplication key` · `also absent: error classification, delay or backoff, jitter, timeout or cancellation`\n\nFrom the code (`src/legacy/payouts.ts:23-42`):\n```ts\nwhile (attempt < 5) {\n  attempt += 1;\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(\"https://payouts.internal/v1/send\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        destination: payout.destination,\n        amountCents: payout.amountCents,\n        at: Date.now(),\n      }),\n    });\n    if (res.ok) return true;\n  } catch {\n    // swallowed: the caller cannot tell a failure from a refusal\n  }\n}\nreturn false;\n```\n\nThree things the scan doesn't put together:\n\n1. **`payout.id` is never sent.** The request body only has destination, amount and a timestamp. The receiver has nothing to deduplicate on, and `at: Date.now()` changes on every attempt, so even \"same body twice\" matching would fail.\n2. **It retries every failure.** Any non-`ok` response (including a 4xx refusal) and any thrown error (including a timeout after the provider already committed) triggers another POST, up to 5 times with no delay.\n3. **The error is swallowed and the function returns `false`** (`crime_00015`, same function). A payout that went through but whose response was lost gets reported as failed. Any caller or operator that reacts by calling `sendPayout` again starts a fresh set of up to 5 POSTs.\n\nThe author knew about it: `// TODO: this needs an idempotency key before the next audit` (line 13), next to `// TODO: nobody has owned this file since the migration` (line 14).\n\n### What a customer would experience\n\nA network blip or slow response from `payouts.internal` after it has already moved the money leads to the same payout being sent again, up to 5 times per call. The money goes out to an external destination. That's real money leaving the business, and pulling it back from a third party is slow and often impossible. Meanwhile our side records the payout as **failed**, so the overpayment doesn't appear in our own records. It surfaces later as a reconciliation gap or a ledger that doesn't balance. A customer on the other side of the platform may also see their payout \"fail\" and get retried by support, which makes it worse.\n\n### Why it outranks what the scan puts near the top\n\nThe scan has `recency_enabled: true`. The top of the list is mostly findings in `src/checkout/*` with `recency: 1`, which reflects what the team is touching now, not danger. `payouts.ts` has `recency: 0`, `test_gap: 0.75` and no owner, which is the reason it sank.\n\n- **`crime_00001`, Basket contract drift (high, ranked #1).** Two `Basket` interfaces disagree on `currency` and `discountCents`. That's a type-level hazard: a future change might drop a discount or currency. It has no runtime failure today and would most likely show up as a wrong display or a compile-time surprise. Blast radius is 2 importers.\n- **`crime_00005`, mixed UTC/local in `reconcile` (high).** Mixing UTC and local time misplaces rows across day and hour buckets depending on the host's timezone. It's a real bug, but it affects internal reconciliation counts. It doesn't move money, and you can fix it and run it again.\n- **`crime_00006`, duplicated `admin && plan !== \"free\"` rule (high).** Both copies are identical today. The risk is future drift, not current harm.\n- **`crime_00010`, `settleBatch` retry (same charge, scored identically to payouts).** This is the closest rival, but it's less bad on every axis. It sends `ledgerId`, so the provider at least has a natural key to deduplicate on. It only re-POSTs on 502/503/504 or a thrown error, not on every non-ok status. It's a settlement against our own payment provider, not a payout to an outside destination. Its swallowed error also doesn't report a committed operation as failed the way `sendPayout` does.\n- **`crime_00020`, swallowed error in `createSession` (recency 1).** A failed checkout session gets hidden. That's bad UX, but nothing is moved twice.\n\nThe scan correctly sees each piece of `sendPayout` separately: unsafe retry, swallowed error, clock read in the payload, and no owner. Scored one at a time, each lands around medium. Together they make the only path in the repo that can **silently send money to an outside party several times while recording that it failed**. The fix is to send a stable idempotency key derived from `payout.id` on every attempt, retry only errors that might succeed on a second try (network failures and 5xx), and stop swallowing the error.",
